\f0\fs22 \cf2 The form was yellow, which meant swimming.\
There was a colour for everything. Blue was excursions, green was anything involving a bus, pink was the one that came home four times a year and went back with money in it. Yellow was water. Sarah had been collecting yellow forms since she was five, the way other children collected stickers, and had never once handed one back.\
This one had been on the kitchen table for eleven days.\
She had filled in her half on the first night, in her best handwriting, the slow kind that made her wrist ache. Name: JACOBSON, SARAH. Class. Age. The box that asked what stroke you would be swimming, which she had thought about for a long time and answered honestly, because she did not know, and had written LEARNING, and then crossed it out because it looked like an excuse, and written FREESTYLE, and gone to bed feeling like a liar and a criminal and, underneath that, wonderful.\
She had also filled in the line marked PARENT OR GUARDIAN (BLOCK LETTERS), because it seemed helpful, and because it was one less thing.\
ANSTEY, J.\
Underneath that was a second box, ruled off with a heavier line, and the second box was the problem. The second box was for a doctor.\
Her mother came in at twenty past eight with her coat still on and grey dust in the turn-ups of her trousers, and looked at the form the way you look at a cat that has brought something in.\
\u8220 ?I have to hand it in tomorrow,\u8221 ? Sarah said. \u8220 ?It\u8217 ?s the last day. Mrs Pinder said.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?Did she.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?It\u8217 ?s just the carnival. It\u8217 ?s not even a real thing, it\u8217 ?s in the afternoon and then we go home.\u8221 ?\
Her mother put her bag down and went to the sink and ran the tap and did not drink anything, which was a thing she did.\
Sarah had planned this. She had planned it for eleven days and had a case, and she made it well, because she was a child who made cases and had learned young that the ones you shouted did not work.\
She had been well for four years. Four years, and she had the evidence: no winters, no Tuesdays at the clinic, no plastic chairs, no forms in any other colour. She had not needed a single thing from anybody. She had not been a bother. She had not asked for the trip to the aquarium or the one with the bus, which she had wanted so much that she had not asked, on the calculation that not asking for two would buy her one. This was the one.\
Her mother said, \u8220 ?No.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?You haven\u8217 ?t even \u8212 ?\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?No, Sarah.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?It\u8217 ?s one afternoon.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?I know what it is.\u8221 ?\
And there it was, the whole shape of it, in the length of the kitchen: a woman who would not say why, and a child who could not be told, and a yellow form on the table between them with a heavy line ruled across the middle of it.\
Sarah went at the line. She had worked out that the doctor\u8217 ?s box was the hinge; she had not worked out anything past that.\
\u8220 ?Ellie\u8217 ?s mum signs hers and they never even go. They just sign it. Mrs Pinder said if a parent signs the top part then the \u8212 ?\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?Mrs Pinder is not a doctor.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?Neither are you.\u8221 ?\
Her mother turned round at that, and Sarah felt the small cold satisfaction of having landed one, and then, immediately, the worse feeling underneath it.\
\u8220 ?I\u8217 ?m not sick,\u8221 ? Sarah said, and her voice came out wrong, higher than she wanted. \u8220 ?I\u8217 ?m not sick any more. You know I\u8217 ?m not. You made me not sick.\u8221 ?\
Her mother did not move.\
\u8220 ?So what was the point,\u8221 ? Sarah said, \u8220 ?if I still can\u8217 ?t do anything.\u8221 ?\
It was the closest either of them ever came.\
Her mother looked at her for about three seconds. Whatever went across her face, Sarah did not have the equipment to read it at eleven and would not have another chance. Then it was gone, and her mother said, \u8220 ?Get the bowl.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?I don\u8217 ?t want to.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?Sarah. Get the bowl.\u8221 ?\
So the argument ended, the way arguments in that house ended, which was that one of them had work.\
The bowl was a white one with a chip out of the rim, and it lived on the third shelf and was not used for anything else, and Sarah had carried it to the table since she was seven without ever being asked twice.\
The ritual had not changed in four years and Sarah could have done it in the dark. The bowl. The kitchen chair pulled out and turned side-on. Sleeve up past the elbow, which she did herself. Her mother\u8217 ?s bag on the table and the flat tin out of it, and the tin opened away from Sarah, always away, so that what was in it was her mother\u8217 ?s business.\
Then the other things, which took longer than the injection and always had. The tape measure round Sarah\u8217 ?s upper arm, and round her chest, and the pencil mark on the doorframe with Sarah standing straight and her heels back. The little torch in each eye. Her mother\u8217 ?s watch held against Sarah\u8217 ?s wrist while she counted, lips moving. The two needles into the vein in the crook of Sarah\u8217 ?s elbow first, the ones that filled, which had hurt when she was seven and did not hurt now, and which went into the tin, and which no clinic ever saw. And in between all of it, the notebook, and the pencil, and her mother writing.\
Sarah had asked once, when she was eight, what she was writing.\
Her mother had said, \u8220 ?Numbers.\u8221 ?\
The rules were older than the ritual. Do not tell anyone. Do not tell Ellie. If you fall over at school and they want to look at you, say you are fine and come home. Do not let anybody take your blood.\
\u8220 ?Not even a nurse?\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?Especially not a nurse.\u8221 ?\
It had been an adventure at seven. At eleven it was Wednesday.\
The medicine had come home from work, and Sarah knew that in the way children know that fathers come from somewhere and money comes from somewhere; the place her mother worked was a long way under the ground and smelled, on her mother, of the shower she had to take before she was allowed to leave it, and Sarah had once said that her mother smelled like a swimming pool and had been told, quite sharply, not to say that at school.\
The medicine had worked in stages, and this was the part Sarah remembered longest, and would still be turning over two hundred years later when there was nobody left to ask.\
First the winters. She was seven and she did not get the winter, and everybody said, well, she\u8217 ?s had a good run, and waited. Then the appointments started falling off the calendar, one at a time, like a queue that stops moving because the office ahead of it has quietly closed. Then everything else. She fell off the low wall at Ellie\u8217 ?s and skinned her whole forearm, and picked at it that night out of habit and found nothing there to pick, only new skin, pink and slightly too smooth, and she had run to show her mother because it was extraordinary and she was seven and everything extraordinary went to her mother.\
Her mother had held Sarah\u8217 ?s arm under the kitchen light for a long time and turned it over twice.\
She had not been pleased. That was the thing Sarah could never get round afterwards. There had been no pleasure in her face at all. There had been something that worked very hard, and something underneath the working that Sarah would only much later have a word for, and then her mother had let go of Sarah\u8217 ?s arm and gone and got the notebook.\
Now, four years on, the tin was open on the table and her mother was not doing any of it in the right order.\
She did the injection first, which she had never once done, and she did not do the arm measurements at all, and she did not get the pencil, and when Sarah looked at her, her mother was already going back into the tin.\
\u8220 ?You did it,\u8221 ? Sarah said.\
\u8220 ?I know.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?You already did it.\u8221 ?\
Her mother took out the rest of them. There were five, and Sarah had never seen more than one out of the tin at a time, and had not known until that moment that there was ever more than one in there.\
\u8220 ?Hold your arm.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?Why?\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?Hold your arm out, Sarah.\u8221 ?\
She held her arm out, because she was eleven, and because her mother had her coat on, and because the last four days her mother had not come home until Sarah was asleep and the two days before that she had not come home at all, and because whatever was happening in the kitchen was very obviously not about the yellow form and Sarah could not work out what else there was for it to be about.\
Her mother did all five. She was quick and she was not gentle and she did not talk, and the fourth one went in badly and left a bruise that would be gone by Friday.\
Then she put the empty tin in her bag, and did not put the bowl away, and did not take the pencil out at all.\
There was no spoon of anything afterwards. There had been a spoon of something afterwards since Sarah was seven.\
Sarah pulled her sleeve down. Her arm was hot from the inside, in five places.\
\u8220 ?Am I in trouble?\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?No.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?It\u8217 ?s because of the form.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?It\u8217 ?s not because of the form.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?Then why are you \u8212 ?\u8221 ?\
Her mother crouched down. She did not do that either. She put both hands on the sides of Sarah\u8217 ?s head, one over each ear, which she had done when Sarah was small and had not done for years, and held her there for a moment, and Sarah could feel the cold of the outside still on her mother\u8217 ?s fingers.\
\u8220 ?I\u8217 ?ll be back for your birthday,\u8221 ? her mother said.\
Then she stood up and picked up her bag and went to work.\
She had left grey dust on the kitchen floor where she crouched, and Sarah, who had eleven days of grievance in her and nowhere to put it, got the dustpan out and swept it up so that it would be gone before morning, and did not think about it again for a very long time.\
The door went at eighteen minutes to nine.\
Sarah went and had a look at the form, which was still on the table, still with the heavy line ruled across the middle of it, and then she went to bed.\
\
The cake was bought early, on the Thursday, because Sarah\u8217 ?s aunt believed in getting in ahead of a crowd.\
By the Saturday there was no reason to buy another one, so they had that one, and it was fine. Sarah had a piece. Her aunt had two and said something about her figure that she plainly did not mean.\
Nobody had rung. Nobody rang the following week either, or the week after that, and there was no letter, and when the aunt finally got somebody at the number on the payslip, the woman on the other end said the department did not exist and had not existed, and then said it again more slowly, as though the fault were in the hearing.\
Sarah went back to school on the Monday. She had not handed the yellow form in and the office would not refund the two dollars fifty because the closing date had passed.\
Her aunt, on the phone, on the subject of the man in the office, said men ain\u8217 ?t nothin\u8217 ? but dicks and hormones, which Sarah wrote down in the back of an exercise book because it was the funniest thing she had ever heard, and which she was still saying two centuries later, at parties, to people who had no idea she was quoting anyone.}
